<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<subsection xml:id="ch7-section-thermodynamics-review-subsection-adiabatic" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title>Lapse rates and adiabatic processes</title>

    <p>
        A temperature <term>lapse rate</term> is the rate of temperature decrease with increasing height.
        In other words,
        it is the rate at which temperature <em>cools</em> with increasing height.
        By this definition,
        a lapse rate is positive (negative) when temperature cools (warms) with increasing height.
    </p>

    <p>
        For a location at geometric height <m>z</m> within Earth's atmosphere,
        the <term>environmental lapse rate</term> is defined as
    </p>

    <p>
        <md xml:id="eqn-environmental-lapse-rate" number="yes">
            \Gamma = -\frac{\partial T}{\partial z}
        </md>
    </p>

    <p>
        (Note: Some sources use <m>\gamma</m> rather than <m>\Gamma</m>.)
    </p>

    <p>
        The environmental lapse rate can be used to characterize special atmospheric layers by their temperature behavior in the vertical.
    </p>

    <p>
        <ul>
            <li>
                <p>
                    A temperature <term>inversion</term> is a layer of the atmosphere in which temperature warms with increasing height,
                    and so <m>\Gamma \lt 0</m>.
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    An <term>isothermal</term> layer is a layer of the atmosphere in which temperature is constant with height,
                    and so <m>\Gamma = 0 \, \text{K} \, \text{m}^{-1}</m> (or any other appropriate unit).
                </p>
            </li>
        </ul>
    </p>

    <p>
        An <term>adiabatic</term> process is one in which a closed thermodynamic system does not exchange energy with its environment via heat
        (although it still can exchange energy with its environment via work).
        Thus, <m>\delta q = 0</m> for an adiabatic process.
    </p>

    <p>
        We apply the adiabatic assumption to the related form of the <m>1^{\text{st}}</m> law of thermodynamics
        <xref ref="eqn-first-law-alt" text="type-global" />,
        divide by <m>\delta z</m>,
        and let <m>\delta z \to 0</m> to produce
    </p>

    <p>
        <md xml:id="eqn-first-law-alt-adiabatic" number="yes">
             0 = c_p \frac{dT}{dz} - \alpha \frac{dp}{dz}
        </md>
    </p>

    <p>
        (The ordinary derivative can be used if we assume the air parcel experiences changes in temperature and pressure due only to vertical movement;
        otherwise, a partial derivative can be used.)
    </p>

    <p>
        Since <m>\alpha = \dfrac{1}{\rho}</m>,
        the second term in <xref ref="eqn-first-law-alt-adiabatic" text="type-global" />
        can be replaced with <m>g</m> by assuming the air parcel is in hydrostatic balance:
    </p>

    <p>
        <md xml:id="eqn-first-law-alt-adiabatic-hydrostatic" number="yes">
             0 = c_p \frac{dT}{dz} + g
        </md>
    </p>

    <p>
        Rearranging <xref ref="eqn-first-law-alt-adiabatic-hydrostatic" text="type-global" />
        to solve for <m>-\dfrac{dT}{dz}</m> gives
    </p>

    <p>
        <md xml:id="eqn-dry-adiabatic-lapse-rate" number="yes">
            \Gamma_d = -\frac{dT}{dz} = \frac{g}{c_p}
        </md>
    </p>

    <p>
        where <m>\Gamma_d</m> [<m>\text{K} \, \text{m}^{-1}</m>]
        is the <term>dry adiabatic lapse rate (DALR)</term>.
        The DALR gives the rate at which a perfectly dry air parcel cools with increasing height as it rises adiabatically
        while remaining in hydrostatic balance.
        (Likewise, it gives the rate at which a perfectly dry air parcel warms with decreasing height as it sinks adiabatically
        while remaining in hydrostatic balance.)
    </p>

</subsection>